WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.879
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:10.711
Often when people think about leading
change or

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:15.892
implementing change in organizations, they
always think at the 30,000 feet.

00:00:15.892 --> 00:00:17.980
They're very abstract.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.090
They usually talk about generalities such
as, how do I communicate?

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.590
How do I engage?

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.726
How do I reach out to people?

00:00:25.726 --> 00:00:32.070
But often, this 30,000 feet orientation
simply means that we're fighting

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:37.610
the campaign of leading change and
implementing change as an air war.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:42.750
By contrast, if you really want to succeed
in embedding change within organizations,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.880
thinking about it just as an air war is
not enough.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.900
You really have to think about it as a
ground war.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.010
And in order to fight that ground war,

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.970
which is going to be messy, which is going
to be surprise laden.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:59.720
You've actually got to have, not only a
plan that gives you confidence, but

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.072
a plan that emphasizes flexibility.

00:01:02.072 --> 00:01:05.275
[MUSIC]

00:01:05.275 --> 00:01:07.070
PRESS is an acronym.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.190
It consists of five letters.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:14.210
Each letter denotes, if you will, a
sub-process of implementing change.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.870
Remember, it's a sub-process and not a
stage.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:22.100
That means you have to think of these five
sub-processes in tandem.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.850
What does PRESS stand for?

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.750
The first sub-process is persuading.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.240
Persuading has to do with, how do you
influence the emotions of people so

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:37.000
that you can change their beliefs, in the
hope that they will actually you know,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.500
generate new behaviors that are much more
relevant to the organization.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.330
The R in the PRESS framework stands for

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:47.500
recruiting support and overcoming
resistance.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:51.890
Any worthwhile change will face
opposition.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:56.160
Of course, individuals oppose change for a
variety of reasons.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.580
But if we understand the target markets
behind change,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.657
we can understand those individual
reasons.

00:02:01.657 --> 00:02:02.469
And here,

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:07.978
we're much more concerned with how groups
of people can stymie and block change.

00:02:07.978 --> 00:02:13.210
So we're interested, if you will, in the
network structure of resistance and

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:16.110
how to break open that resistance in
organizations.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.280
The third element of the press framework
is E, and it is very important.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.320
It's actually about energizing new
behavior.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.980
A very powerful way in which we can change
the beliefs of

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.090
people is getting them to do things.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.590
And energizing is all about getting people
to do things given how you supply them

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.408
information, given how you cleverly design
rewards, or empower individuals.

00:02:39.408 --> 00:02:44.420
The other sub-process in the PRESS
framework is staffing.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.470
Staffing actually has to do with, how do
you put together a change team?

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.630
And here, who should be on the change
team?

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:56.130
What does that actually convey in terms of
credibility of the change initiative, and

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:56.890
so forth?

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.180
And we'll talk about how the change team
may actually be related to

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.841
the executive committee or the executive
leadership of the enterprise, and

00:03:06.841 --> 00:03:10.350
how various teams can bud off from the
change team

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.620
to implement sub-tasks that are part of
the overall change.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.750
The final S, of course, is sequencing.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.800
Sequencing has to do with the ordering of
the actions, and these actions may

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.770
have to do with persuading or energizing
or recruiting or staffing.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:30.490
Figuring out when to do what is important.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:34.850
So, what we'll actually focus on in the
sequencing module is,

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.770
what do we need to do before day one of
the change?

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.200
What do we need to do on day one?

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.480
What do we need to do by day ten, day 20?

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.210
We'll also build an opportunity for
review.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:49.152
And if you have a plan like that, the
chief consequence is confidence.

00:03:49.152 --> 00:03:53.803
But it'll also give you again, opportunity
for review and editing, and

00:03:53.803 --> 00:03:58.453
then you can actually see how much of
persuasion do you want to emphasize,

00:03:58.453 --> 00:04:01.542
how much of energizing do you want to
emphasize?

00:04:01.542 --> 00:04:05.048
And what is it you want to do to ensure
that,

00:04:05.048 --> 00:04:10.864
as you're implementing the change, there
is momentum rather than fatigue?

00:04:10.864 --> 00:04:13.383
There is excitement rather than cynicism.

00:04:13.383 --> 00:04:23.279
[MUSIC]

